{% extends "tutorial_base.html.j2" %}

{% block tutorial_body %}
        <!-- Intro Card -->
        <div class="doc-intro-card">
          <h2>From Multi-Tube CAD Weldment to Multi-Stick G-Code</h2>
          <p>
            Real-world structural fabrications—such as roll cages, chassis frames, handrails, and headache racks—contain dozens of tubes across several cross-section sizes in a single STEP file. RotaryCAM automatically classifies every solid by cross-section profile, filters compatible parts for the stock currently loaded in your rotary chuck, flips and rolls tubes so angled miters and fishmouth copes interlock tightly end-to-end across raw sticks, optionally merges matching ends into a single shared common-line sever cut, and sequences cuts from the free end toward the chuck so your tube stays rigidly supported throughout the entire program.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-intro-grid">
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Profile Grouping</span>
              <span class="value">Automatic AI Cross-Section Match</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Packing Algorithm</span>
              <span class="value">Interlocking 1D + Common-Line</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Cut Sequencing</span>
              <span class="value">Free-End → Chuck (Rigid Support)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 1
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 01</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Load the <code class="mono">Headache Rack [COMPLEX]</code> Multi-Body Fixture</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Start from the left sidebar's <strong>Quick CAD Fixtures</strong> panel (or upload your own multi-body <code class="mono">.step</code> assembly).
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip">Headache Rack [COMPLEX]</span> in the <strong>Quick CAD Fixtures</strong> list.</li>
              <li>RotaryCAM parses all <strong>37 individual solids</strong> in the assembly, extracting cross-section dimensions, lengths, and cut features in seconds.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 1 — Clicking 'Headache Rack [COMPLEX]' in the Quick CAD Fixtures panel</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/nesting-1-fixture.png" alt="Step 1: Selecting Headache Rack [COMPLEX] in Quick CAD Fixtures" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 2
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 02</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Switch to the <code class="mono">Multi-Part Pack Nesting (37 PARTS)</code> Workspace</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Once the 37-part assembly loads, the full rack structure appears in the top-left <strong>Entire Model Overview</strong>. To nest parts onto raw stock sticks:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click the <span class="ui-chip">Multi-Part Pack Nesting (37 PARTS)</span> tab at the top of the center workspace, <em>or</em></li>
              <li>Click the blue <span class="ui-chip cyan">Open Pack</span> button inside the <strong>Multi-Part Pack Nesting</strong> card on the left sidebar.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 2 — Opening the Multi-Part Pack Nesting workspace (37 detected parts)</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/nesting-2-open-pack.png" alt="Step 2: Clicking the Multi-Part Pack Nesting (37 PARTS) tab or Open Pack button" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 3
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-3">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 03</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Open the <code class="mono">CUTTING PROFILE</code> Cross-Section Selector</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Because a single STEP assembly often mixes several different tube sizes (e.g., <code class="mono">38.1 × 38.1 mm</code> main frame posts alongside <code class="mono">25.4 × 25.4 mm</code> louvers and <code class="mono">OD 14.3 mm</code> round bars), RotaryCAM groups parts by their cross-section profile so only matching tubes are nested onto the same physical stick.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click the <span class="ui-chip">CUTTING PROFILE</span> dropdown bar near the top of the nesting workspace (currently showing <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect (38.1x38.1mm) (7 parts)</code>).</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 3 — Clicking the CUTTING PROFILE dropdown to choose which stock size to nest</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/nesting-3-profile-selector.png" alt="Step 3: Highlighting the CUTTING PROFILE selector bar in Multi-Part Pack Nesting" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 4
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-4">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 04</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Select <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect (25.4x25.4mm) (17 parts)</code></h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              The dropdown lists every unique cross-section detected in the CAD assembly along with the exact part count for each profile:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect (38.1x38.1mm) (7 parts)</code> — 1.5" × 1.5" square HSS frame tubes</li>
              <li><span class="ui-chip">Profile_HSS_Rect (25.4x25.4mm) (17 parts)</span> — 1.0" × 1.0" square HSS crossbars &amp; louvers</li>
              <li><code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Round (OD 14.3mm) (2 parts)</code> — round tube inserts</li>
              <li>Select <span class="ui-chip">Profile_HSS_Rect (25.4x25.4mm) (17 parts)</span> to filter the nesting table and automatically select all 17 matching parts.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 4 — Selecting 'Profile_HSS_Rect (25.4x25.4mm) (17 parts)' from the detected profiles</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/nesting-4-select-profile.png" alt="Step 4: Dropdown showing detected tube profiles and selecting Profile_HSS_Rect (25.4x25.4mm) (17 parts)" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 5
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-5">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 05</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Inspect Multi-Stick Packing (<code class="mono">99.8% Efficiency</code>) &amp; Click <code class="mono">⚡ Compile Pack G-Code</code></h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              RotaryCAM's 1D nesting solver immediately arranges all <strong>17 pieces</strong> (<code class="mono">7480.3 mm</code> total cut length) across <strong>2 sticks</strong> of <code class="mono">6000 mm</code> raw stock with a <code class="mono">10.0 mm</code> kerf/part spacing gap:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Stick 1 of 2 Preview Bar:</strong> Packs 7 parts (<code class="mono">Part 10</code>, <code class="mono">Part 9</code>, <code class="mono">Part 34</code>, <code class="mono">Part 36</code>, <code class="mono">Part 4</code>, <code class="mono">Part 24</code>, <code class="mono">Part 35</code>) totaling <code class="mono">5987 mm / 6000 mm</code>—leaving only <code class="mono">13 mm</code> of remainder (<span class="ui-chip emerald">99.8% EFFICIENCY</span>). Use the <code class="mono">◀ Prev Stick</code> / <code class="mono">Next Stick ▶</code> buttons to inspect Stick 2.</li>
              <li><strong>Automatic Interlocking Miter &amp; Cope Nesting (180&deg; Flip + A-Axis Roll):</strong> You never have to waste triangular scrap wedges between angled tube ends. RotaryCAM automatically tests 180&deg; end-for-end flips and A-axis roll rotations on every tube in the pack so adjacent miters and fishmouth copes tuck tightly into each other.</li>
              <li><strong>Optional Single-Cut Common-Line Severing:</strong> Check <strong>Common-line sever cuts</strong> to merge matching 90&deg; square cutoffs or complementary miter joints into a single shared sever cut with an on-kerf pierce—cutting your cutoff pierces in half and eliminating scrap rings between parts.</li>
              <li><strong>Customizable Quantities, Spacing &amp; Seams:</strong> Adjust <strong>Part Spacing (Gap)</strong> (<code class="mono">10 mm</code>), <strong>Stock Tube Length</strong> (<code class="mono">6000 mm</code>), enable <strong>Auto-tab pack seams</strong> to keep pieces joined on the stick, or increment part quantities (<code class="mono">- / +</code>) in the table below.</li>
              <li>Click the green <span class="ui-chip emerald">⚡ Compile Pack G-Code</span> button at the top of the nesting panel to generate the multi-stick 4-axis G-code programs.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-callout tip" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">✓ How Interlocking Miters &amp; Common-Line Sever Cuts Save Stock &amp; Consumables</div>
              <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                When nesting mitered frame rails or fishmouth-coped roll cage tubes, keeping parts in their raw CAD orientation leaves dead triangular air gaps between pieces. By automatically testing 180&deg; end-for-end flips and A-axis roll angles on every tube, RotaryCAM nests matching angles face-to-face. Turning on <strong>Common-line sever cuts</strong> takes it one step further: wherever two adjacent parts meet at a matching 90&deg; cutoff or complementary miter, the torch pierces once on the kerf line and severs both parts in a single pass—saving half your cutoff pierces and leaving zero loose scrap rings on the table.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 5 — 17 parts packed across 2 sticks (99.8% efficiency on Stick 1) and clicking Compile Pack G-Code</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/nesting-5-compile.png" alt="Step 5: Multi-Stick arrangement bar showing 99.8% efficiency and Compile Pack G-Code button" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 6
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-6">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 06</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Simulate Stick 1 / Stick 2 in 3D &amp; Download Per-Stick <code class="mono">.nc</code> Programs</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Once compiled, RotaryCAM automatically switches to the <strong>3D Cut Simulation</strong> view and populates the right-hand <strong>G-Code Post-Processor Terminal</strong> with dedicated tabs for each physical stick:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Switch &amp; Download Stick G-Code (Top-Right Panel):</strong> Click between <span class="ui-chip">Stick 1: pack_stick_1.nc</span> and <span class="ui-chip">Stick 2: pack_stick_2.nc</span>, then click <span class="ui-chip emerald">⬇ Download pack_stick_1.nc</span> to save the G-code file for your CNC controller.</li>
              <li><strong>Multi-Stick 3D Simulation (Center Viewport):</strong> Use the <span class="ui-chip cyan">◀ Stick 1 of 2 ▶</span> selector in the simulation toolbar and click <span class="ui-chip">▶ Play</span> to verify the entire 6000 mm stick cutting sequence—ordered automatically from the free end (<code class="mono">X = -5991.78 mm</code>) back toward the rotary chuck (<code class="mono">X = 0 mm</code>).</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-callout tip">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">✓ Why RotaryCAM Cuts from Free-End to Chuck</div>
              <p>
                When cutting multiple parts out of a long 20ft / 6000 mm tube held in a single rotary chuck, RotaryCAM always sequences features starting at the farthest free end (<code class="mono">Piece 1/7</code>) and works progressively toward the chuck. Each finished part drops off cleanly only after all of its internal cutouts and end miters are cut, keeping the remaining tube rigidly clamped in the chuck right up to the final cut.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout info" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">📦 One-Click Multi-Stick Export: Download All Sticks (.zip)</div>
              <p>
                When nesting large multi-part assemblies that span across several 20ft / 6000 mm mill sticks, clicking the <strong>Download All Sticks (.zip)</strong> button bundles every compiled G-code program (<code class="mono">pack_stick_1.nc</code>, <code class="mono">pack_stick_2.nc</code>, <code class="mono">pack_stick_3.nc</code>, etc.) into a single organized archive.
              </p>
              <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                Copy the single <code class="mono">.zip</code> to your shop USB drive or machine console to run each stick in sequence without downloading files one-by-one or mixing up stock runs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 6 — Selecting Stick 1 / Stick 2 tabs and downloading pack_stick_1.nc</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <img src="assets/nesting-6-download.png" alt="Step 6: Downloading pack_stick_1.nc and switching sticks in 3D Machine Simulation" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>
{% endblock %}
